# Copy to infra/deploy/.env and fill in (docs/deploy.md, First start). Never commit the filled-in file.
# On a new server the preflight fills in the secrets for you:
#   node scripts/preflight.mjs --install --fill-secrets
# When a release adds a required setting whose name ends in _SECRET, _PASSWORD or _TOKEN, it's a long
# random string, and the updater makes one up and adds it here itself (docs/upgrade.md, The updater).

# The AUGUR release to run, such as 0.1.1 (GitHub, Releases, augur@<version>).
# Upgrading is changing this, then pulling (docs/upgrade.md).
PLATFORM_VERSION=
# Optional: where the images come from, for a mirror in your own registry.
# PLATFORM_IMAGES=ghcr.io/harry-burton-dev/im-platform

# Where the platform will live: https://<TS_HOSTNAME>.<your tailnet>.ts.net
# (your tailnet name is in the Tailscale admin console under DNS).
PLATFORM_URL=https://platform.example-tailnet.ts.net
TS_HOSTNAME=platform
# A Tailscale auth key (Settings → Keys → Generate auth key; reusable is easiest).
TS_AUTHKEY=tskey-auth-...

# Long random strings; `openssl rand -hex 32` makes one.
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=
KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Each platform service's and Keycloak's own database login (the storage job makes them). POSTGRES_PASSWORD
# above is the admin login, which only the storage job and backups use.
IDENTITY_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
REGISTRY_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
EVENTS_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
MODELS_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
KEYCLOAK_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
# Apps' file storage (Garage): its nodes' shared secret and its admin API's token.
GARAGE_RPC_SECRET=
GARAGE_ADMIN_TOKEN=
# The registry's key to the bucket apps' frontends are kept in by version (64 hex). Without it each app
# is served from wherever its manifest says, and holding an app at an older version doesn't hold its code.
FRONTENDS_BUCKET_SECRET=
# Optional: the key every app's database, bucket and sign-in secrets are worked out from, so apps the
# updater installs need nothing in this file (docs/app-storage.md). Keep it as safe as POSTGRES_PASSWORD.
# APP_SECRETS_KEY=
# With APP_SECRETS_KEY and the updater, the updater also runs apps (docs/upgrade.md, The app runner).
# Who else may sign the apps it installs, besides this repository's Release workflow: owner/repo, space separated.
# APP_PUBLISHERS=
# The Docker network the app runner puts apps on (default: the updater's own, usually platform_default).
# APPS_NETWORK=
# Each platform service's own sign-in (identity makes them in Keycloak).
IDENTITY_CLIENT_SECRET=
REGISTRY_CLIENT_SECRET=
EVENTS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# The model gateway's sign-in; needed before models can be used (Agents).
MODELS_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Folio, the document libraries every deployment ships with: its sign-in, its database login,
# and its bucket's secret (64 hex: `openssl rand -hex 32`).
FOLIO_CLIENT_SECRET=
FOLIO_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
FOLIO_BUCKET_SECRET=
# Auspex, where you are on AUGUR, which every deployment ships with too: its sign-in.
AUSPEX_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Data Atlas, the org's entities and how they connect, ships with every deployment too: its sign-in,
# its database login, and its bucket's key (made for every app; Data Atlas keeps nothing there).
ATLAS_CLIENT_SECRET=
ATLAS_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
ATLAS_BUCKET_SECRET=
# Mandate, intent passed down the floors, ships with every deployment too: its sign-in, its database
# login, and its bucket's key (made for every app; Mandate keeps nothing there). On a server whose app
# runner installed Mandate before, the updater fills them with the secrets the runner gave it.
MANDATE_CLIENT_SECRET=
MANDATE_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
MANDATE_BUCKET_SECRET=

# You: the first person who can sign in (Keycloak needs every one of these), such as jsmith, Jo, Smith.
# You'll be asked to change the password on first sign-in.
PLATFORM_ADMIN_USER=
PLATFORM_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
PLATFORM_ADMIN_EMAIL=
PLATFORM_ADMIN_FIRST_NAME=
PLATFORM_ADMIN_LAST_NAME=
PLATFORM_ADMIN_DEPARTMENT=Administration
# Optional: Keycloak's own admin. The realm job (keycloak-config) signs in as it on every `up` to set the
# realm up, and it's the way back in if every admin loses their second step (docs/deploy.md). Its password
# is KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_PASSWORD above: if you change it in Keycloak's console, change it here too.
# KEYCLOAK_ADMIN_USER=keycloak-admin
# Optional: sign-in rules stricter than AUGUR's floors (ADR 0038). Looser values are ignored, with a
# warning in `pc logs keycloak-config`. REALM_PASSWORD_DENYLIST names a file in
# infra/deploy/keycloak/password-blacklists (default ncsc-top100k.txt; `none` turns the check off).
# REALM_PASSWORD_MIN_LENGTH=15
# REALM_SESSION_IDLE_HOURS=8
# REALM_SESSION_MAX_HOURS=10
# REALM_SIGN_IN_EVENT_DAYS=90
# REALM_ADMIN_EVENT_DAYS=365
# REALM_PASSWORD_DENYLIST=ncsc-top100k.txt
# Optional: the languages the sign-in pages and emails offer, comma separated, the first the default
# (default en). Any language Keycloak has, such as de, fr or pt-BR; others are left out with a warning.
# REALM_LANGUAGES=en
# Optional: what the organisation's own floor is called (default Organisation). The sign-in pages and
# emails show it too.
# ORG_NAME=
# Optional: a link to the organisation's logo, shown at the top of the sign-in pages (none by default).
# ORG_LOGO_URL=
# Optional: the product's name, in the browser tab, on the sign-in pages and in emails (default AUGUR).
# ORG_PRODUCT_NAME=

# Optional: the organisation's SMTP relay (docs/deploy.md, "Email"). Without SMTP_HOST AUGUR sends no
# email: no password resets or address checks, and screens say so. SMTP_FROM is required with a host.
# SMTP_HOST=
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_SECURITY=starttls
# SMTP_FROM=
# SMTP_REPLY_TO=
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=

# Optional: sign in with the organisation's Entra account (docs/deploy.md, "Entra"). All three, or none.
# ENTRA_TENANT_ID=
# ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=
# ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Nightly backups land in infra/deploy/backups. Optional: when (hour, UTC) and how many days to keep.
# BACKUP_HOUR_UTC=2
# BACKUP_KEEP_DAYS=14
# The weekly restore test runs after the backup on this day (0 is Sunday).
# RESTORE_TEST_WEEKDAY=0
# Off-server copy (ADR 0060; docs/deploy.md, "Off-server copy"): each night's backup also goes, encrypted
# here first, to a restic repository in an S3-compatible bucket this deployment names. Until it's set,
# Admin → Status says backups stay on this server. The bucket's key should be able to add but not erase
# (see the guide). Keep a copy of OFFSITE_PASSWORD off this server too: without it the copy can't be read.
# OFFSITE_REPOSITORY=s3:https://s3.example.org/augur-backups/workbench
# OFFSITE_PASSWORD=
# OFFSITE_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# OFFSITE_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# OFFSITE_REGION=
# How many days of copies the bucket keeps.
# OFFSITE_KEEP_DAILY=30
# Restore to a point in time (ADR 0064; docs/deploy.md, "Restore to a point in time"): Postgres archives
# its changes as it goes, and each night's base backup is what they're replayed onto. How many days back
# that reaches (base backups kept, here and in the off-server copy), and at most how many seconds of
# changes wait before they're archived.
# PITR_KEEP_DAYS=7
# PITR_ARCHIVE_SECONDS=900

# Optional: the model behind Agents and apps' AI (see docs/agents.md). Any OpenAI-style API:
# OpenRouter (https://openrouter.ai/api/v1), a LiteLLM proxy, or "stand-in" for a model that
# doesn't think, to try things out. Without it, Agents stay off.
# MODEL_API_URL=https://openrouter.ai/api/v1
# MODEL_API_KEY=
# MODEL_NAME=openai/gpt-4o-mini
# The model that turns text into vectors, for matching by meaning (Data Atlas's Triage), if the API has one:
# MODEL_EMBEDDING_NAME=openai/text-embedding-3-small
# How long to wait for the model to answer, in seconds.
# MODEL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60

# Optional: other apps that keep their own database schema and files here, space separated, such as
# "notes" (Folio's are made without being named). Each needs <ID>_DATABASE_PASSWORD and <ID>_BUCKET_SECRET (64 hex, `openssl rand -hex 32`),
# where <ID> is the app id in capitals with _ for -. See docs/app-storage.md.
# APP_STORAGE=
# NOTES_DATABASE_PASSWORD=
# NOTES_BUCKET_SECRET=

# Optional: limits at the front door (docs/deploy.md). Requests a second from one address and the burst
# let through at once, for the API and for sign-in; and the largest upload, in MB (each app has its own
# limit under it).
# API_RATE_PER_SECOND=30
# API_RATE_BURST=200
# SSO_RATE_PER_SECOND=10
# SSO_RATE_BURST=50
# MAX_UPLOAD_MB=2048
# Optional: Keycloak's own admin console (PLATFORM_URL/sso/admin) is closed to the network. Set to open
# while you change realm settings by hand, then remove it and `pc up -d` again.
# KEYCLOAK_CONSOLE=open

# Optional: how much Docker keeps of each container's log (docs/deploy.md, "Logs, memory and health").
# LOG_MAX_SIZE=10m
# LOG_MAX_FILES=3
# Optional: memory limits for the containers that can take a lot.
# KEYCLOAK_MEM_LIMIT=2g
# FOLIO_WORKER_MEM_LIMIT=4g
# OTEL_LGTM_MEM_LIMIT=2g

# Optional: other sites apps may load their code from, space separated, such as
# https://apps.example.org. Apps served by the platform itself never need this.
# Each one must also be allowed in Admin, Apps, "Where apps load from".
# APP_ORIGINS=

# Optional: where the shell and every app load the UI kit from (docs/deploy.md, "The UI kit"). By default
# this server serves it itself, at /kit/. To put a CDN in front, name any https address that serves the
# same files at the same paths (mirroring PLATFORM_URL/kit/) with a CORS header for PLATFORM_URL. The
# browser checks every file against the hash this release pinned, so nothing else can be served there.
# KIT_URL=/kit/

# Optional: how the shell and every app draw what's behind a dialog, a menu or the palette (docs/deploy.md,
# "Desktops without a GPU"). veil or lens blur it; scrim only dims it, for desktops without a GPU (virtual
# desktops), where blur is slow. Unset, each app chooses, which is veil unless it says otherwise.
# FOCUS_DEPTH=

# Optional: let an admin fill this deployment with synthetic sample people, floors, documents and
# entities (pnpm seed, docs/seed.md). For dev, staging and demo servers only; never where real people work.
# SEED_ALLOWED=true

# This deployment's read key, while the repository is private: a GitHub token with only read:packages,
# saved as infra/deploy/secrets/github-read-key (owned by user 1000), and the account it belongs to.
# With it, Admin, Upgrade Centre and Apps check GitHub for new AUGUR and app releases, and the updater pulls
# them (docs/upgrade.md, "The read key").
# GITHUB_READ_USER=

# Optional: Admin, Upgrade Centre checks for new releases once a day, from GitHub. Point it at a mirror's
# feed instead (an https:// address, or file:///run/augur/releases/releases.json for one kept in
# infra/deploy/releases; docs/mirror.md), or set it to off.
# RELEASE_FEED_URL=
# Optional: where new versions of apps are listed (default: GitHub, beside the release feed; off by
# itself when RELEASE_FEED_URL is set), or off.
# APP_FEED_URL=
# What kind of server this is (docs/release-channels.md): dev, the workbench you build on, which runs
# what's on dev; preview, which runs what's on its way to production and may follow preview releases and
# pull requests' builds; or production, which takes only releases promoted to production. Set here and
# not in Admin, so no admin session can change it.
SERVER_RING=production
# Optional: stable, beta or dev. By default dev (AUGUR's dev builds, taken by itself) on the workbench, beta on a
# preview server and stable on a production one, which never follows anything else.
# RELEASE_CHANNEL=
# Optional: the time zone upgrade windows are in (Admin → Upgrade Centre), such as Europe/London. UTC by default.
# On production, AUGUR's patch releases install by themselves on Sunday at 02:00 in it, unless you change that there.
# DEPLOYMENT_TIME_ZONE=
# The Upgrade Centre's line from dev to production comes from the pipeline feed beside the release feed
# (docs/release-channels.md, "The pipeline feed"); off to hide it. Off by itself with a mirror.
# PIPELINE_FEED_URL=
# The company's Backstage, which the Upgrade Centre links to, such as https://backstage.<tailnet>.ts.net.
# PORTAL_URL=
# Optional: this server's name in Backstage's catalog (org/deployments.yaml), such as augur-workbench. With it,
# the registry signs a record of what this server runs and sends it to PORTAL_URL after each change and once
# a day, or, without PORTAL_URL, lets an admin export it from the Upgrade Centre (docs/upgrade.md, "Reporting
# to Backstage"). No personal data is in it. off turns reporting off.
# DEPLOYMENT_ID=
# DEPLOYMENT_REPORTING=
# Optional, on a production server: on runs pull requests' builds for apps' developers there too.
# ALLOW_PR_PREVIEWS=
# Optional, only for AUGUR published from a fork: who must sign its release feed, its images and its dev
# builds, as certificate identity patterns (docs/upgrade.md, "The signed feed"; docs/release-channels.md,
# "Signatures"). The publisher's own workflows when unset. AUGUR_DEV_SIGNER follows IMAGE_SIGNER's repository.
# FEED_SIGNER=
# IMAGE_SIGNER=
# IMAGE_SIGNER_ISSUER=
# AUGUR_DEV_SIGNER=

# Optional: how many days apps' domain events are kept for followers to catch up on (docs/domain-events.md).
# DOMAIN_EVENTS_KEEP_DAYS=30
# Optional: how many days closed inbox items are kept (docs/inbox.md).
# INBOX_KEEP_DAYS=90
# Optional: how many days back the Inbox's Done tab looks, at most INBOX_KEEP_DAYS (docs/inbox.md).
# INBOX_DONE_DAYS=30
# Optional: the key the gateway signs callers with (64 hex, `openssl rand -hex 32`). Without it the gateway
# makes a new one each start, which is fine for one gateway (docs/signed-caller.md).
# CALLER_SIGNING_KEY=

# Optional: the updater, which applies an upgrade asked for in Admin, Upgrade Centre (docs/upgrade.md,
# "The updater"). Turn it on with the profile, give it this folder's full path on the server and a
# sign-in secret, then `pc up -d`.
# COMPOSE_PROFILES=updater
# PLATFORM_DIR=/home/you/augur/infra/deploy
# UPDATER_CLIENT_SECRET=
# Before read keys: the account a read:packages token in secrets/image-pull-token belongs to.
# Not needed with GITHUB_READ_USER and secrets/github-read-key.
# IMAGE_PULL_USER=

# How the deployment watches itself (docs/observability.md). Every AUGUR service, and the shell's browser
# errors, go over OTLP to the deployment's own otel-lgtm, whose Grafana runs AUGUR's alert rules. To send
# to your organisation's collector instead, name it here and set OTEL_LGTM_REPLICAS=0; OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=true
# turns telemetry off altogether.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_ENDPOINT=http://otel-lgtm:4318
# OTEL_LGTM_REPLICAS=1
# OTEL_SDK_DISABLED=
# Your own collector's key, as headers (key=value, comma separated), and which traces to keep, such as
# parentbased_traceidratio with 0.1 for one in ten.
# OTEL_EXPORTER_OTLP_HEADERS=
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER=
# OTEL_TRACES_SAMPLER_ARG=

# Optional: how long one service waits for another before it says that one can't be reached (default 5).
# SERVICE_CALL_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=5
# Optional: how long one database query may run before Postgres cancels it (default 60; 0 for no limit).
# DATABASE_STATEMENT_TIMEOUT_SECONDS=60

# Required: the password for Grafana's admin, at least 16 characters (openssl rand -hex 16).
GRAFANA_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
# Grafana's key to tell deployment admins of an alert, through the registry (`openssl rand -hex 32`).
# The updater makes it up on upgrade.
ALERT_WEBHOOK_TOKEN=

# Optional: the base map for Data Atlas's maps, a PMTiles file you put in ./maps (docs/atlas.md, "Maps"),
# and the credit its data needs (OpenStreetMap's when empty).
# ATLAS_MAP_TILES=/maps/region.pmtiles
# ATLAS_MAP_ATTRIBUTION=
